1. The Classic Run-Down (What Russell Actually Showed)
In naive set theory (late 1800s, Cantor/Frege), the rule was simple:
Any well-defined property defines a set.
This is unrestricted comprehension:
For any formula φ(x), there exists a set { x | φ(x) } — no restrictions.
Russell asked (1901):
Consider the property:
"x is a set that does not contain itself as a member"
(in symbols: x ∉ x)
Define R as:
R = { x | x is a set and x ∉ x }
→ R is the set of all sets that do not contain themselves.
Now the killer question:
Is R a member of itself? (R ∈ R?)
Two possibilities — both lead to contradiction:

Case 1: Assume R ∈ R (R contains itself)
→ By definition, R only contains sets that do not contain themselves.
→ So if R is in R, then R must not contain itself.
→ Contradiction: R ∈ R and R ∉ R.
Case 2: Assume R ∉ R (R does not contain itself)
→ By definition, R contains every set that does not contain itself.
→ So if R does not contain itself, then R should be in R.
→ Contradiction: R ∉ R and R ∈ R.

Boom — Russell's paradox.
R can neither be in R nor not be in R.
The definition is logically impossible.
This is why naive set theory is inconsistent — it allows contradictions to be true, and in classical logic, contradiction implies anything (ex falso quodlibet). The whole system collapses.
2. Traditional Fix: "R Cannot Exist" (ZFC Set Theory)
The mainstream solution (Zermelo-Fraenkel + Choice, ZFC, 1908 onward) says:
R is not a set — it never existed.
They prevent it by restricting comprehension:

You can only form subsets from existing sets (axiom of separation).
No universal set of all sets exists (it's a "proper class").
Self-membership loops are banned (axiom of foundation/regularity).

So R is "blocked" — you can't collect all non-self-containing sets into one object.
R is an exception because it cannot be a set.
The paradox is avoided by saying "the definition is invalid" — no set is formed.
This works great for 99% of math — it's consistent, powerful, and builds everything from numbers to topology.
But it feels like a patch — a restriction to avoid the explosion, not an embrace of the deeper truth.
